# TabBar — a strip of tabs that are ROUTES, which is the one thing the language's
# own `tabs` node is not.
#
# `tabs bind cell:` is a switch: it owns a client cell, holds every panel's nodes
# inside itself, and swaps which one renders. That is right for a view of the same
# data (For you / Following) and wrong for a section of a site: the panels are
# whole pages with their own data, their own titles and their own addresses, and
# folding them into one route means none of them can be linked, bookmarked or
# opened in a new tab. A TabBar is a row of NavLinks that happens to look like
# tabs; which one is current comes from the route, not from a cell.
#
# So this facet is a container and nothing else. It ships no tab component of its
# own — the tab IS a NavLink, restyled by being inside this row — because a
# `TabLink` would be NavLink's markup, NavLink's current-state rule and NavLink's
# route-expression handling written a second time, and the second copy is the one
# that stops matching. The rules below reach into `.x-navitem` deliberately: the
# container owns the arrangement, the atom owns the item.
#
#     use TabBar():
#         use NavLink("Posts", "/u/" + handle)
#         use NavLink("Replies", "/u/" + handle + "/replies")
#
# THE DESTINATIONS ARE CONCATENATED, NOT INTERPOLATED, and this example used to
# get it wrong. A component argument is an EXPRESSION: the quoted-and-braced form
# is a string literal containing braces, and it used to compile clean and render
# `href="/u/{handle}"` — a link that goes nowhere, with no diagnostic. The compiler
# refuses it now. `+`
# concatenates when either side is text and converts the other, so the value
# arrives as a value and `Link` interpolates it into the node. A call site that is
# WRITING the path rather than passing one along should prefer the language's own
# `link "Posts" -> "/u/{handle}"` node, where the template form is checked against
# the app's routes and a typo is a build failure.
#
# Neither line says which tab is current: `NavLink` compares the destination to
# `route` itself now, so a tab strip written once in a shared layout marks the tab
# you are on — which is the only thing a tab strip is for, and the thing it could
# not do while `current` was an argument. A strip whose tabs cover subtrees rather
# than single pages uses `NavLinkWhen` with a `contains(route, …)` per tab.
import "navlink.fct"
app FacetNav:
    css:
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tabbar { display: flex; flex-direction: row; align-items: stretch;
                   gap: 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--x-line, #2f3336);
                   border-radius: 0; padding: 0; background: none; }
        # Equal thirds, quarters, whatever there are: a tab strip whose widths
        # follow the label lengths re-flows every time a count in a label changes.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tabbar .x-navitem { flex: 1; width: auto; justify-content: center;
                        border-radius: 0; padding: 16px 8px; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600;
                        border-bottom: 4px solid transparent; }
        # The current tab is underlined rather than filled, because the strip sits
        # on a border and a filled tab and a hovered tab read the same.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tabbar .x-navitem-current { border-bottom-color: var(--fa-accent); }
    component TabBar():
        row class "x-tabbar":
            slot
